P = "/home/hatch/workspace/apex-audit/docs/university/data/courses/_il29-build/batch-F1.json"

EXT = {
("IL29-M41", 0): """ A detail worth internalizing: the phases are drawn as a cycle because real incidents refuse to stay in one box. You contain three hosts, then firewall logs reveal a fourth beaconing host you missed — you are back in detection and analysis, re-scoping, before containment resumes. The current revision of the guidance is NIST SP 800-61 Revision 2, and its core message has not changed across revisions: coordination and preparation matter more than tools. Another worked micro-example: during eradication of the ransomware case, the team discovers the phishing email also reached the billing department's shared mailbox, which auto-forwards to an external accountant. That single finding re-opens scoping (is the accountant's machine compromised?), extends containment (block the forwarding rule), and adds a post-incident action item (review all mailbox forwarding rules quarterly). One artifact, three phases touched. Operator gotcha for the exam-minded: when a question asks 'which phase,' look for the verb — building, testing, and documenting are preparation; confirming and scoping are detection and analysis; isolating and blocking are containment; removing root cause is eradication; restoring service is recovery; reviewing and reporting are post-incident. The verbs decide.""",

("IL29-M41", 1): """ Two preparation artifacts separate mature teams from improvisers: the jump bag and the tested restore. The jump bag — physical or virtual — holds everything the technical lead needs in the first hour: a laptop with forensic tools, write-blocker, external drives, clean OS media, console cables, and the printed phone tree. Build yours before you need it; during the incident is the wrong time to discover your USB drive is corrupted. On backups, preparation means proving the restore, not assuming it: schedule monthly restore tests of a sample system and a sample file share, record the result (success, duration, data integrity check), and store the log where the incident team can find it at 2 AM. A backup that has never been restored is a hope, not a control. Also prepare your evidence handling in advance: designate where forensic images go (an encrypted external drive in the safe, with a custody log taped to it), decide your hashing standard (SHA-256), and confirm you have legal's guidance on what to preserve if the incident might involve employee misconduct. Final gotcha: preparation decays. People change roles, phone numbers change, tools get replaced. Put a six-month expiry on the contact list and the playbook review, with a named owner — otherwise your beautiful plan is three years out of date when the alert fires.""",

("IL29-M41", 2): """ Dwell time — the gap between initial compromise and detection — is the statistic that should haunt you. Industry reports have historically measured dwell in weeks or months for incidents discovered by third parties; your goal is to drive yours toward hours through better detection and user reporting. Practically, scoping is where analysis lives or dies. Beyond checking which users opened the attachment, pull firewall and DNS logs for the indicators: which internal hosts resolved the malicious domain, which hosts made the outbound connection, and when did each start. One clinic case: the EDR showed three encrypted hosts, but DNS logs showed eleven hosts resolving the attacker's domain over the prior week — the true scope was eleven, and the extra eight were caught before encryption because someone looked at DNS. Another analysis discipline: separate the signal from the noise in the first 30 minutes by assigning one person to timeline duty while others contain — the timeline builder records every confirmed fact with a timestamp, which prevents the team from acting on rumors. Common tuning mistake: after a noisy false positive, teams silence the rule instead of tuning it. Silencing deletes future true positives; tuning (adding the legitimate admin tool's path as an exception, narrowing the condition) keeps the detection alive. Every alert disposition — true positive, benign true positive, false positive — should feed back into detection engineering, which is preparation for the next incident.""",

("IL29-M41", 3): """ Containment decisions vary by host role, and the playbook should say so in advance. A user workstation: isolate aggressively, minimal business impact. A file server: isolate, but coordinate — taking it offline halts the whole clinic, so the incident commander weighs encryption spread against business halt, and the answer is usually 'isolate now, the encryption will halt the business worse.' A domain controller: do not isolate blindly — authentication for the entire network depends on it; instead block the malicious outbound traffic at the firewall, disable compromised accounts, and monitor closely while planning a controlled rebuild. These are decisions you want pre-made, not invented at 3 AM. Communication during containment follows the same discipline: the communications lead sends timed updates ('containment in progress, next update at 04:00') to a defined distribution list, and nobody else freelances status reports. A second containment gotcha: watch for the attacker's counter-move. Ransomware crews monitor for containment — when hosts go dark, they may accelerate encryption on remaining hosts or trigger pre-planted persistence. That is why containment should be as simultaneous as possible across the scoped host set rather than trickled one machine at a time. Document the containment timestamp per host; the post-incident review will compare 'scope at containment' versus 'final scope' to judge whether containment was fast and complete enough.""",

("IL29-M41", 4): """ Credential resets deserve their own eradication sub-plan, done in tiers. Tier 1 — known-compromised accounts (the phished user, any account seen in lateral movement): reset immediately during containment. Tier 2 — privileged accounts (domain admins, service accounts): reset during eradication even without direct evidence of compromise, because credential theft is quiet and the cost of missing one is another incident. Tier 3 — all remaining user passwords: forced reset during recovery, communicated clearly so the help desk is not drowned. Service accounts need special care: resetting them without updating every dependent service causes outages, so inventory service accounts during preparation, not during the incident. Validating backups before recovery is equally tiered: confirm the backup timestamp predates the earliest known malicious activity (not just the encryption date — dwell matters), verify integrity with a test restore of a non-critical system first, and scan restored data with current detections before returning it to production. Enhanced monitoring after recovery should be specific, not vibes: EDR in heightened sensitivity on recovered hosts for 30 days, daily review of authentication logs for the first two weeks, firewall alerts on any outbound connection to the previously seen malicious infrastructure, and a weekly scope re-check for the first month. Eradication is only verified when the monitoring period ends clean — declare victory then, not on recovery day.""",

("IL29-M41", 5): """ The after-action report has two audiences and should be written for both: a one-page executive summary (what happened, business impact, what we are changing) and the technical appendix (full timeline, artifacts, indicators). Distribute the technical findings beyond the IR team — detection engineering needs the indicators, IT operations needs the hardening tasks, and training needs the user-facing lesson. Track every action item to closure in whatever system the organization actually checks; an action item that lives only in the AAR document is decorative. Review the metrics quarterly across incidents: is MTTD trending down? Are repeat root causes appearing (the third phishing incident this year means the email control, not the users, is the problem)? Feed systemic findings into the risk register so leadership sees incident lessons as risk reduction, not just IT homework. A final post-incident discipline: the 'pre-mortem' for the next incident. Ask the team, 'It is six months from now and we are breached again through the same vector — what did we fail to do after this incident?' The answers become your highest-priority action items, because they represent the lessons you are most likely to let slide. Incident response maturity is not measured by how well you handle the incident — it is measured by how few of your action items are still open at the next tabletop.""",

("IL29-M41", 6): """ Facilitation tips that separate a useful tabletop from a pleasant chat: keep the scenario plausible but uncomfortable — the exercise should find the gap, not confirm the plan. Time-box each inject (15-20 minutes of discussion) and cut off rambling with 'noted — what is the decision?' Inject information asymmetrically the way real incidents do: the technical lead learns the EDR is down before the incident commander does, forcing a communication test. Include at least one inject that invalidates the plan ('the phone tree's first three numbers go to voicemail') because plans always fail at the seams. For remote teams, run the tabletop over the actual out-of-band channel you would use in an incident — testing the bridge line is part of the exercise. Evaluation is observation, not grading: the facilitator and scribe watch for decision latency (how long before someone declares an incident?), authority clarity (did anyone actually make the call, or did the group drift?), communication discipline (did updates flow through the comms lead?), and evidence thinking (did anyone say 'preserve memory before wiping'?). Convert every observation into a playbook edit within a week, and re-run the same scenario in six months to verify the fixes stuck. One exercise per quarter, rotating scenarios (ransomware, business email compromise, insider data theft, third-party breach notification), builds the muscle memory that makes the real 2 AM call feel familiar instead of chaotic.""",

("IL29-M42", 0): """ Put numbers on the decay to make the ordering instinctive. RAM: total loss at power-off, partial corruption within minutes of heavy system activity as memory gets reused. Network state (ARP cache, active connections): entries age out in minutes; DHCP leases and NAT tables churn constantly. Running processes: the list is current only while the system runs — reboot and you lose which processes were active, their PIDs, and parent-child relationships. Disk: file timestamps update on access and modification, logs roll over on busy systems within days, and unallocated space gets overwritten unpredictably. Remote logs: the most durable, but only if forwarding was configured before the incident. The live-versus-dead decision matrix: system is ON and you suspect fileless activity, credential theft, or active intrusion — treat as live, capture volatile evidence first. System is OFF and was shut down cleanly — treat as dead-box: do not boot it, image the disk write-blocked. System is ON but the incident is clearly over (old malware, user already reimaged once) — targeted artifact collection from the live system is acceptable, documented. And the documentation rule that underpins all of it: every collection action gets a timestamped log entry as you perform it, not reconstructed afterward. 'I think I imaged it around 2' is not a custody record; '14:22 — began FTK Imager acquisition of \\.\PhysicalDrive0 to EVIDENCE-01' is.""",

("IL29-M42", 1): """ Two companion artifacts make MFT analysis far stronger: $LogFile and $UsnJrnl. The NTFS log file ($LogFile) records transactional metadata changes — file creations, deletions, renames — and can reveal activity whose MFT record was later reused. The Update Sequence Number Journal ($UsnJrnl) logs file changes with timestamps and reasons (creation, deletion, rename, data overwrite), giving you a second witness when MFT timestamps look tampered. Together, MFT + $UsnJrnl let you catch timestomping red-handed: the journal says the file was created Tuesday while SI claims 2019. On resident versus non-resident data: small files and alternate data streams may live inside the MFT record itself (resident), which means they survive even when the file's clusters are long overwritten — malware configuration blobs hidden in ADS are recoverable this way. Large files are non-resident: the MFT stores data runs (cluster addresses), and carving follows those runs across the disk. Worked micro-example: an MFT record for invoice.pdf shows SI timestamps of last month but the $UsnJrnl entry for the same file reference number shows creation yesterday with reason 'file create' — the file is new, the timestamps are forged, and the journal entry is your proof. Common tooling note: parse the MFT with a proper parser (many free ones exist) rather than eyeballing hex — the attribute layouts are intricate, and misreading an attribute offset produces confident nonsense. Always work from the image, and always cross-check a surprising MFT finding against at least one other artifact before building conclusions on it.""",

("IL29-M42", 2): """ Two more high-value sources complete the Windows artifact picture. Sysmon, when installed, logs process creation with full command lines and hashes, network connections per process, and driver loads — it is the single richest endpoint telemetry source on Windows, and deploying it fleet-wide via GPO is one of the highest-value preparation tasks you can do. Without Sysmon, enable 4688 process-creation auditing with command-line logging as the fallback. Amcache.hve deserves special attention: it records executed programs with their first-execution timestamps, file paths, and SHA-1 hashes, often going back months further than prefetch — when prefetch has rolled over, Amcache frequently still has the answer. For USB investigations, pair USBSTOR with the setupapi.dev.log (device install timestamps) and the Security log to build connection windows. And the preparation lesson that dwarfs all artifact knowledge: centralize your logs before the incident. Windows Event Forwarding (WEF) or a SIEM collecting Security, System, and PowerShell logs means the evidence survives even if the attacker wipes the local logs — which, per ATT&CK T1070.001, is one of the first things intruders do. Size the retention for your event volume: a domain controller generating 50,000 events a day with a 20 MB log keeps roughly hours of history. That sizing math belongs in preparation, decided once, not discovered during triage when the logs you need rolled over yesterday.""",

("IL29-M42", 3): """ The acquisition workflow in practice: arrive, photograph, then run the memory tool from external media with output directed to the same external media — the command line, tool version, start and end times all go in your notes. Expect roughly one minute per gigabyte on modern hardware; a 16 GB laptop takes 10-20 minutes, during which the machine must stay powered and undisturbed. If the machine is locked, do not attempt to log in as the user (you will change last-logon artifacts and potentially trigger the attacker's awareness); capture memory from the lock screen state — the RAM contents are what matter, not the desktop. Beyond full RAM, know your partial sources: pagefile.sys holds paged-out memory in no particular order — strings carved from it can yield passwords, URLs, and chat fragments months old. hiberfil.sys on laptops is a compressed full-RAM snapshot from the last hibernation — analysts have recovered entire intrusion sessions from it. The swapfile and memory compression stores on newer Windows builds add further fragments. Analysis begins with the basics: process list (look for processes with no name, odd parents, or injected threads), network connections at capture time, and loaded DLLs per process. Then strings analysis on dumped process memory — attacker infrastructure, commands, and stolen data often sit in cleartext. Anti-forensic awareness: sophisticated malware may detect acquisition tools or wipe on shutdown, which is one more reason memory comes before any other action. And the legal note: memory can contain other users' private data (open documents, messages) — handle the image with the same custody and access controls as any sensitive evidence.""",

("IL29-M42", 4): """ Tooling for timelines ranges from manual to automated, and you should understand both. The manual method — spreadsheet, one row per artifact, UTC-normalized — is slow but teaches you what each artifact actually means, which is why this module's lab uses it. The automated method follows the super-timeline concept: tools like Plaso (log2timeline) parse dozens of artifact types into a single sorted bodyfile. Automation is fast and comprehensive, but it is garbage-in-garbage-out: feed it a misconfigured timezone and every row is wrong with great confidence. Whichever method you use, the interpretation discipline is the same: cluster related rows (the five events within 90 seconds of the malicious download are one action, not five mysteries), flag anomalies (timestamps out of order, gaps where logs were cleared), and write the narrative in plain sentences before concluding. Timelines also exonerate: 'the user was accused of exfiltrating data at 3 PM, but the timeline shows their workstation was locked from 1 PM and the transfer came from a service account' — that finding matters as much as any guilty one. Timezone handling in detail: record every source's native timezone assumption (event logs: UTC internally; prefetch: local system time as FILETIME UTC; browser history: varies), convert everything to UTC once, and state the conversion in your report. When the timeline spans multiple machines, verify each machine's clock offset — a workstation five minutes fast can invert cause and effect. A timeline without documented timezone handling is a story, not evidence.""",

("IL29-M42", 5): """ Custody continues after collection: store images on encrypted media in a locked location with restricted access, log every access (who, when, why), and re-verify hashes whenever the image is copied — a hash checked at collection and never again proves nothing about the copy you analyzed six months later. For incidents that might become HR or legal matters, establish a legal hold early: written instruction to preserve specified evidence, suspending normal retention-deletion for those items, with counsel's involvement. The hold prevents the classic disaster of the backup rotation overwriting the key evidence mid-investigation. Documentation standards that survive scrutiny: contemporaneous notes (written during, not after), exact commands with versions, source identifiers (make, model, serial, asset tag), and hash values recorded in at least two places (your notes and the evidence label). Photograph physical evidence in place before moving it — the sticky note with the password on the monitor is evidence of the user's practice, and its position matters. When transferring evidence, use a handoff log: giver, receiver, date, time, hash verification at transfer. Common failure modes: the 'trusted colleague' handoff with no log ('I gave the drive to Dave'), analyzing from the only copy (always work from a verified duplicate, keeping a pristine master), and storing evidence on a network share with broad access (defense counsel will ask who else could have touched it — 'everyone in IT' is a bad answer). Custody is boring until the day it is the entire case.""",

("IL29-M42", 6): """ The communication around reimaging matters as much as the technical steps. Tell the user what is happening in plain words: 'Your machine shows signs of compromise, so I am saving diagnostic data before rebuilding it clean — this takes about 20 minutes, then you will have a fresh system.' That single sentence prevents the 'IT took my laptop and will not say why' resentment cycle. Give a realistic return time and keep it; if analysis will take days, say so and provide a loaner. After reimaging, validate before returning: confirm the build came from known-good media (verify the image hash if your process supports it), confirm EDR and patching are active, restore only user data (documents, not executables or installers) from a backup predating the incident, and reset the user's password with MFA re-enrollment. Then close the loop back into detection: the preserved artifacts should produce at least one improvement — a blocked domain, a new detection rule, a GPO change, or an awareness note. The reimage checklist is therefore not the end of the incident but a handoff: preservation → rebuild → return → improve. Track the metric that proves the runbook's value: mean time from 'suspected compromise' to 'preserved and rebuilding.' When leadership asks what the security program delivers, 'we preserve evidence on every suspected host within 30 minutes and every case improves a control' is an answer backed by process, not adjectives. And keep the runbook itself under version control with a review date — the checklist that nobody updates quietly becomes the checklist nobody follows.""",

("IL29-M43", 0): """ Security researcher David Bianco's Pyramid of Pain organizes indicators by how much pain changing them causes the attacker — and it is the best mental model for why IOAs beat IOCs. At the bottom (trivial to change): hashes, IPs, domains. In the middle: network and host artifacts, tools. At the top (painful to change): tactics, techniques, and procedures — the adversary's actual tradecraft. Your detection program should climb the pyramid deliberately: block the easy IOCs automatically, but invest analyst time at the TTP level, because that is where detections survive the attacker's next build. Sharing intelligence follows the same logic. IOCs are shared as simple lists and feeds; richer context travels in structured formats like STIX/TAXII, and frameworks like ATT&CK give both sides a common language so 'we saw T1059.001 from a Word macro' means the same thing to every team. A practical sharing example: after your ransomware case, you publish an internal bulletin with the phishing sender domain (IOC, expires in 30 days), the malicious hash (IOC), and the behavior chain — Office macro → encoded PowerShell → LSASS access → scheduled task (IOAs, kept as detections permanently). The IOCs age out; the behavior detections keep paying. One caution on consuming external intel: every feed contains false positives and stale entries. Before blocking a feed IOC fleet-wide, check age, source confidence, and whether the artifact could be shared infrastructure (a compromised legitimate site is not the same as an attacker domain). Intelligence is evidence to evaluate, not orders to execute.""",

("IL29-M43", 1): """ ATT&CK comes in matrices for different domains: Enterprise (Windows, macOS, Linux, cloud, containers — the one you will live in), Mobile, and ICS. Within Enterprise, platforms let you filter techniques to your reality — a Windows fleet cares about a different subset than a cloud-native shop. Navigating the knowledge base is a skill: start from the tactic pages to learn the story, drill into techniques for detection and mitigation guidance (each technique page lists data sources, detection ideas, and mitigations — free consulting from the people who catalogued the adversary), and check the procedure examples to see how real groups implement it. Note the version: ATT&CK updates regularly, techniques get renamed, split, or deprecated, so cite the version in your documentation the way you would cite any reference. A second core concept worth naming: ATT&CK also catalogues mitigations and data sources per technique, which makes it a defensive planning tool, not just an attacker encyclopedia. When your coverage map shows a blind spot on T1053.005 Scheduled Task, the technique page itself tells you which logs to collect and which mitigations apply. Common beginner trap: treating ATT&CK as a compliance checklist ('we must cover all techniques') — it is a knowledge base for prioritization, not a standard to certify against. The goal is risk-informed coverage of the techniques most likely used against your environment, defended in depth, with the reasoning documented. That reasoning — why these techniques, why these controls — is what a panel respects, not the percentage covered.""",

("IL29-M43", 2): """ A second walkthrough cements the pattern — this time a cloud-flavored intrusion, to show the tactics travel. Initial Access: valid credentials bought from an infostealer log (T1078 Valid Accounts) — no phishing needed. Execution: the attacker runs commands through the cloud provider's own management console. Persistence: a new federated identity provider trust (cloud equivalent of a scheduled task). Defense Evasion: deleting cloud audit logs. Credential Access: dumping the identity store. Discovery: enumerating storage buckets and roles. Lateral Movement: assuming roles across accounts. Collection and Exfiltration: copying snapshots to an external account. Impact: deleting the originals. Same tactic story, different techniques — which is exactly why tactic-level thinking transfers across environments while technique specifics change. From the defender's chair, each tactic suggests a control family: Initial Access → phishing-resistant MFA and conditional access; Execution → application control; Persistence → change monitoring on startup mechanisms; Credential Access → credential guarding and tiered admin; Lateral Movement → segmentation; Exfiltration → egress monitoring and data classification. Work that mapping for your own environment once, and you have a security architecture justified in adversary language rather than vendor language. The interview version of this walkthrough takes 90 seconds: pick the endpoint ransomware chain, name each tactic as you go, and land on the punchline — 'and that is why blocking at Execution, before persistence and credential access, is the highest-leverage control we have.' Brevity with structure signals mastery; rambling through fourteen tactics signals memorization.""",

("IL29-M43", 3): """ Calibrate confidence explicitly, because stakeholders act on your certainty. Three levels work in practice. High: the behavior has almost no legitimate explanation — encoded PowerShell spawned by Word, LSASS accessed from an unsigned binary. Say so, and act. Medium: suspicious but plausibly legitimate — PowerShell downloading a file could be an admin script or a download cradle; confirm with the user or the change record before declaring. Low: consistent with attacker behavior but equally consistent with normal operations — a new scheduled task might be software updater or persistence; investigate, do not alarm. Write the confidence into the ticket alongside the mapping: 'T1059.001, high confidence — no legitimate workflow on this host spawns encoded PowerShell from Office.' That one line lets the next analyst, the manager, and the auditor all move at the right speed. Then turn mappings into detections: every confirmed malicious mapping is a candidate for a permanent rule. The Word-spawns-PowerShell mapping becomes 'alert on any Office application spawning a script interpreter, fleet-wide' — an IOA-based detection born from one incident, protecting against every future variant. Keep a mapping journal as suggested, but review it quarterly: patterns in your mappings reveal your environment's real threat profile. If 80% of your mappings are Initial Access via phishing and Execution via macros, your security roadmap writes itself — email controls, application control, user reporting — and you can defend the budget with your own data instead of industry fear statistics.""",

("IL29-M43", 4): """ Timing and restraint are part of stakeholder communication. Tell executives early but briefly — 'we detected and blocked an attack attempt on one workstation at 09:15; no data affected; investigation underway; next update at noon' — because they should never learn about security events from rumors. Tell users what affects them and what to do, nothing more; a clinic-wide 'we had a cyber incident' email teaches everyone to ignore future messages and occasionally tips off the insider you have not found yet. Tell auditors and compliance with precision and timestamps after the facts are firm. And know what not to say: never speculate about attribution ('it looks Russian') — you almost certainly do not know, and the speculation will be quoted. Never promise 'no data was exfiltrated' until the investigation supports it — say 'no evidence of exfiltration to date; investigation continues.' Never discuss the incident on social media or with reporters unless you are the designated communications lead with a prepared statement. A worked micro-example of restraint done right: the help desk asks 'was it ransomware?' in the ticket thread. Accurate answer: 'We blocked a malicious document attempting code execution — consistent with the early stage of ransomware chains. No encryption occurred. Scoping in progress.' That sentence is truthful, appropriately alarming, and contains zero speculation. Common failure: the analyst who briefs the executive with technique IDs and log excerpts — watch the eyes glaze over, then watch trust erode. Translate, then offer the technical appendix for anyone who wants depth. Communication is a control: it determines whether the organization responds as a team or as a rumor mill.""",

("IL29-M43", 5): """ Presenting the coverage map to leadership is where the exercise pays for itself. One page, three colors: green (prevent), yellow (detect), red (blind) across the techniques most relevant to your environment. Executives do not need all fourteen tactics — show the eight that matter for an endpoint fleet and the story they tell: 'We prevent the common execution techniques, we detect credential theft, but we are blind to persistence mechanisms and lateral movement — here is what each blind spot costs us and what closes it.' Attach a cost and a timeline to each fix: 'Enable scheduled-task auditing via GPO: zero license cost, two weeks, closes persistence blindness.' That framing turns the map into a funded roadmap. Score the map quarterly and trend it — 'blind techniques down from 9 to 4 this year' is a security program metric leadership understands. Link each improvement back to incidents and near-misses: 'This detection would have caught the March phishing attempt at the execution stage instead of the encryption stage.' And keep the map honest about 'detect': a technique is only yellow if the alert exists, is tuned, routes to someone, and has been tested — untested detections are red with better marketing. The final maturity step: use the map for tabletop design. Your blind spots are your scenario injects — 'the attack uses scheduled-task persistence, which we cannot currently see; how do we respond?' — which closes the loop between assessment, exercise, and improvement in one continuous motion.""",

("IL29-M43", 6): """ Tie ATT&CK fluency to STAR stories from your own experience, because the panel hires on evidence, not vocabulary. 'Tell me about a time you handled a security alert' becomes: Situation — 'EDR flagged encoded PowerShell from Word on a workstation.' Task — 'Determine if it was malicious and scope it.' Action — 'I mapped it to Execution T1059.001 with Defense Evasion indicators, checked the delivering email, found it reached twelve inboxes, and had IT purge it while I verified no other host executed it.' Result — 'One host reimaged, zero spread, and I wrote the detection rule that now alerts on any Office-spawned interpreter fleet-wide.' Eighty seconds, behavior to mapping to outcome to improvement — that is the whole module in one answer. Prepare three such stories (a block you investigated, a mapping that found a blind spot, a stakeholder explanation that worked) and you can answer most technical-behavioral questions by reaching for one. Also prepare two questions for THEM that show operator thinking: 'How does the team currently map detections to ATT&CK — is there a coverage map I would contribute to?' and 'What does the escalation path look like when a block might be a false positive affecting clinical operations?' Questions like these signal you think about their real problems, not just your interview performance. Final trap to avoid: never inflate. If your ATT&CK experience is this course plus lab work, say so proudly — 'I have mapped fifty scenarios in training and built coverage maps for a fictional clinic; I am fluent in the workflow and honest about the boundary of my experience.' Panels can smell bluffing; they reward the candidate who knows exactly what they know.""",

("IL29-M44", 0): """ Three phishing variants deserve names because they change the defense. Spearphishing targets a specific person with researched detail — the invoice from your actual vendor, the email from your actual manager (possibly from their compromised account). Whaling targets executives with authority-flavored lures — the fake board memo, the 'urgent acquisition' wire request. Business email compromise (BEC) often has no payload at all — just a conversation: 'Are you available? I need a favor,' followed by gift-card or wire instructions. BEC defeats every attachment scanner ever built because there is nothing malicious to scan — the defense is purely procedural (verify out-of-band, require dual authorization for payments). A newer variant: QR-code phishing ('quishing') — the malicious link hides inside a QR code the email scanner cannot easily read, and the victim scans it with a phone that may lack the company's email protections. Defenses adapt: QR codes in email get the same suspicion as links, and phones get the reporting habit too. Attachment discipline in plain terms: treat unexpected attachments as guilty until verified — Office macros, HTML attachments, ISO/ZIP containers, and especially multi-stage 'open the HTML, click the link, enter credentials' chains. Worked micro-example of BEC defended: the CFO's 'urgent wire' email arrives Friday at 4:55 PM. Procedure: no wire moves on email instruction alone — the finance staffer calls the CFO on the known number. Ninety seconds, attack dead. That procedure, written down and followed under pressure, is worth more than any email filter against BEC.""",

("IL29-M44", 1): """ Two emerging twists raise the stakes on voice and SMS fraud. AI voice cloning lets an attacker reproduce a trusted voice — a manager, a family member — from seconds of public audio, turning 'verify the voice' from a defense into a vulnerability. The countermeasure is a shared secret or callback procedure agreed in advance: 'any unusual request gets verified by calling back on the known number, no matter how real the voice sounds.' Families use a code word; companies use the callback rule. SIM swapping is the heavier variant: the attacker convinces the carrier to move your number to their SIM, intercepting calls and SMS codes — which is why SMS-based MFA is the weakest form and app-based or hardware MFA is the upgrade path worth pushing. On smishing, the current lure catalog: package delivery ('customs fee due'), toll and DMV notices, bank fraud alerts, and 'wrong number' conversation starters that build rapport over days before the ask (pig-butchering style). The defense pattern never changes: the message supplies urgency and contact details; you supply independent verification. Organization-level defenses: publish the absolute rules ('IT never asks for codes,' 'the bank never texts links'), put the real contact numbers where people can find them in the moment (intranet banner, wallet card, lock-screen wallpaper for shared devices), and run a vishing drill the way you run phishing simulations — a scripted fake IT call to a sample of staff, with kind debriefing. People who have heard the script once recognize it forever; people who have only read about it often do not.""",

("IL29-M44", 2): """ Elicitation — the conversational side of pretexting — deserves its own study because it targets helpfulness, not gullibility. The elicitor never asks for the secret directly; they ask around it: 'I am new here, who handles the VPN issues?' → 'Oh, Marcus does, marcus.j@...' → each answer builds the org chart for the next attack. Techniques include flattery ('you clearly know how things really work here'), feigned ignorance ('I am so lost with this new system'), and the false statement ('so the backups run on Fridays, right?' — corrected to the true schedule by a helpful employee). Defenses: the 'need-to-know pause' (before answering, ask 'why do they need this and who are they?'), and deflecting to public channels ('that is on our website' or 'let me connect you with our office manager'). Physical security drills make this concrete: hire or role-play a friendly visitor attempting tailgating and information elicitation, then debrief — the receptionist who challenged the visitor becomes the example, praised publicly. Visitor policy in plain terms, posted at the desk: every visitor signs in, wears a badge, is escorted beyond the lobby, and the Wi-Fi password is never given verbally to unverified visitors. And the clean-USB rule with its reasoning explained: 'Unknown USB devices go to IT unopened — not because we distrust you, but because attackers count on curiosity, and IT has safe ways to check.' Rules with reasons get followed; rules without reasons get resented and bypassed.""",

("IL29-M44", 3): """ Inoculation theory — the research behind effective awareness training — says that exposing people to weakened versions of manipulation techniques builds resistance, the way a vaccine uses a weakened virus. That is the scientific case for phishing simulations and vishing drills done kindly: each safe exposure, followed by immediate teaching ('here is the lever they pulled'), strengthens the real-world response. It is also the case against fear-only training, which produces anxiety without skill. Frame training around the attacker's economics to demystify: phishing is industrial — templated kits, rented infrastructure, measured conversion rates. The attacker does not need to fool everyone; a 5% click rate on 10,000 emails is 500 footholds. Your defense does not need perfection either — it needs reporting fast enough that one click becomes one contained incident instead of one dwell-time disaster. Teach the 'scam anatomy' pattern across channels so the skill transfers: whether it is email, call, text, or visitor, the shape is the same — unsolicited contact + authority or urgency + a request that bypasses normal procedure + contact details they supply. 'Verify out-of-band' defeats all four uniformly, which is why it is the one habit worth repeating until it is reflex. A final psychology note for trainers: lead with respect. 'Attackers are professionals running an industry, and their techniques work on everyone including security people — here is how we beat them together' outperforms 'do not be the weak link' on every measure that matters, starting with attendance.""",

("IL29-M44", 4): """ Delivering the session remotely or to shift workers takes adaptation, not dilution. Remote: keep cameras encouraged but not mandatory, use polls for the 'phish or legit?' practice (everyone votes, then you reveal — participation without spotlight anxiety), and record for the night shift with the quiz as the attendance check. Shift workers: run the same 30 minutes at shift change twice rather than one 60-minute session nobody attends. New hires: the session belongs in week-one onboarding, before they develop inbox habits — plus a gentle simulated phish in week three as the inoculation dose. The handout design matters more than it seems: one page, large type, the three habits as numbered steps, the reporting path as a giant button-like box, and the after-click steps in a calm color. It lives taped to monitors and saved to phones — the thing people actually look at in the panicked moment. Reinforcement beats repetition: a monthly two-line 'scam of the month' email (one real example, one lever named, one reminder of the reporting path) keeps the habit warm between quarterly sessions far better than repeating the full training. And measure the session itself: a three-question pulse survey ('I know how to report a phish: yes/no,' 'I know what to do after clicking: yes/no,' 'one thing I will do differently: ___') tells you whether the 30 minutes worked and what to fix. Training that is never evaluated is a ritual; training with a feedback loop is a program.""",

("IL29-M44", 5): """ Executive sponsorship turns awareness from an IT chore into an organizational norm. When the clinic director opens the session with ninety seconds — 'I take this seriously, I do the simulations too, reporting is always safe here' — attendance, attention, and reporting all move. Ask for that sponsorship explicitly and make it easy: draft the ninety seconds for them. Reward structures beat punishment structures: a quarterly 'best catch' recognition (the reporter whose flag caught a real campaign), team reporting-rate leaderboards framed as collaboration, and visible follow-through ('because twelve of you reported this, we blocked it for everyone'). The help desk needs its own briefing, because reporters call them first: give the desk a one-page triage card (isolate? collect? escalate to whom? what to tell the user?) so the reporting loop does not die at the first human handoff. Integrate awareness with onboarding, offboarding, and role changes — access changes are the moments attackers exploit, so the human controls should move with them. And connect the program to the incident response plan: user reports should feed the detection phase explicitly ('user-reported phish' as a detection source in the IR playbook), with SLAs for triage. When a user's report triggers the IR process and the user hears back that their report mattered, you have closed the loop between awareness training and incident response — the two modules of this course shaking hands. That closed loop is the difference between a compliance checkbox and a living defense.""",

("IL29-M44", 6): """ Go one level deeper on honest statistics, because leadership will ask whether the program 'works' and you need a real answer. Significance basics: with 200 employees and a 10% click rate, the margin of error is roughly ±4% — so a move from 12% to 9% between quarters is noise, while 24% to 11% is signal. Say this out loud in the report; it builds credibility and prevents overreaction to wobbles. Control for difficulty explicitly: keep a small library of rated lures (easy/medium/hard, rated by a pilot group) and always compare within the same band. Track cohorts longitudinally: the same employees over time, not just company averages — averages hide the ten repeat clickers behind 190 improvers. Watch for the metrics that indicate gaming: reporting rates that fall while click rates fall (people hiding clicks), or suspiciously perfect scores from one department (answer-sharing). And correlate with reality: the ultimate metric is not simulation performance but real-world outcomes — user-reported real phish per quarter, mean time from user report to containment, and incidents with user-report as the detection source. When you can say 'user reports detected 40% of our real phishing incidents this year, up from 10%, with average report-to-containment under an hour,' you have proven the program in the only currency that matters. Present that alongside the simulation trends, keep the no-punishment rule sacrosanct, and the program funds itself — because nothing sells security investment like the workforce demonstrably functioning as a sensor network.""",
}

def main():
    mods = json.load(open(P))
    by_id = {m["id"]: m for m in mods}
    for (mid, idx), text in EXT.items():
        note = by_id[mid]["notes"][idx]
        note["body"] = note["body"] + text
    with open(P + ".tmp", "w") as f:
        json.dump(mods, f, indent=1, ensure_ascii=False)
    import os
    os.replace(P + ".tmp", P)
    # report word counts
    mods = json.load(open(P))
    for m in mods:
        wc = [len(n["body"].split()) for n in m["notes"]]
        print(m["id"], "notes:", len(wc), "min:", min(wc), "max:", max(wc))

if __name__ == "__main__":
    main()
